   kwatson@netcom.com  |  me one or two hundred thousand years to think
   HEx: KNNTA          |  it over.     - KLW, 1993

Addictive drugs such as caffeine, nicotine, and heroin induce lack of
control in the addict because their metabolism has mutated into a form
which cannot function in equilibrium without the drug.  Hence vast
numbers of people who "need" a cup of coffee, a cigarette, a fix.

There is a categorization of drugs called "psychologically addictive"
which IMHO is a bunch of crapola.  The person is still physically in
control and can still function without the drug.

Drug hysteria leads to the lumping together of all illicit drugs into
the same category of "bad things".  Heroin and LSD both strike fear in
the hearts of middle American housewives.

But the fact is, certain consciousness-altering drugs have a place in
the scientific/phenomenological study of the mind.  It is instructive
to examine the effects of LSD and the like on the senses etc.  However, 
use of these under less than ideal circumstances can adversely affect the 
normal survival-preserving behavior that humans have evolved.  These
drugs can only be safely used when proper precautions are taken, starting
with a thorough reading of material on the subject by Leary, Lilly, Hoffman,
et. al., and in the presence of an experienced observer.

There are other ways to get similar changes in the state of consciousness
e.g. Yoga, Tai Chi, "Mind Games" by Masters and Houston.  But these either 
require years of study or extreme concentration of effort to achieve.  So 
use of psychedelic drugs can provide a taste of or introduction to altered 
states,  which are most safely pursued through various mental disciplines,
which I personally prefer.  But without this introduction,  many people 
would not then take the next step and study one of these mental disciplines.

As both a musician and a participant in the AI industry,  I have found
the exploration of altered states to be indispensible.

"Smart drugs" are another issue entirely,  but only raise possible toxicity
concerns as opposed to the extreme care that needs to be taken for the safe
use of psychedelics.  I have no experience with them but do have interest
in the experiences of others.

BTW, It's great to see someone really take seriously certain extropian
principles ignored or touched on rarely by louder members of the list who
are all too often convicted by their convictions.

Andy






