Date: Wed, 17 Nov 93 14:59:54 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECONOMY: There Is No Recession

I was just talking to a noted Extropian on the phone, and he mentioned
the "current recession."

Folks, we are not in a recession, nor are we in a depression. The
so-called "Bush recession," which allegedly began about three years
ago, at the time of the Gulf War, is not a recession, at least not a
conventional one.

While this may be little consolation to the many Extropians who are
unemployed, underemployed, or are facing very bleak employment prospects
when and if they graduate from school, the fact is that the current
situation goes much deeper than mere recession.

What we are seeing is a massive restructuring of the economy. The jobs
"lost" in this so-called recession are for the most part never coming
back. That needs to be said. 

Actually, this realization is dawning on many people (though I've been
saying this for years, so I'm not just a recent convert...I've been
saying this since the '86 "recession"). "Time" magazine, I think it
is, has a cover story on this.

My first essay to the Extropians list, "The Wealth of Ghettoes," dealt
partly with this issue. 

I know many friends who are unemloyed or are barely getting by, while
other friends are making $150K a year and are being asked to take on
even more work! (Granted, $150K a year means you ought to be taking on
a lot of work. What I mean is that they are being offered higher
salaries still due to their skills...)

Partly this is coming from the heavy load of mandated benefits: it
makes little sense for a business to hire an extra body when it can
ask those it already employs to work overtime....no new health
benefits, no retraining, less chance of discrimination lawsuits caused
by the hiring process, etc.

The 10% at the top in Silicon Valley command incredible salaries. I
expect this will soon be the top 8%, then the top 5%, and so on.

I'm not slamming capitalism, of course, just noting what I think is a
very real trend.

Many of the underemployed need to look to new ways of making money.
The Net should offer some real opportunities. Jim Warren, the
columnist and founder of several landmark publication and
conferences, told me at Hackers that the going rate for "Net surfers"
who can use Gopher, WWW, WAIS, etc. to dig up stuff for companies is
upwards of $100 an hour. (Of course, $100 an hour is the "de facto"
cited hourly rate...how many people consistently get it is another
matter.)

Time to realize the economy is changing. A good thing, too. 

This can be depressing news to those awaiting the fabled "end of the
recession," but the truth should not be depressing to Extropians.

--Tim May

