From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: INVEST: Corn futures (was Re: Efficient Market Hypothesis...)

It is worth pointing out that it is not always easy to judge whether a
particular series of experiences provides evidence against the efficient
market hypothesis. Consider Perry's co-op. Let us suppose that, for the
last ten years, they have bought their oil in the summer as a contract for
winter delivery, and that each time they did better than if they had bought
it when they wanted it.

At first glance, it looks as though the odds on their doing that well by
chance are (1/2**10)=1/1024, which is pretty strong evidence. But that
assumes that the payoff is symmetric--equal probability of gaining or
losing. It might not be.

Suppose that, for some reason, the seasonal pattern of oil prices involves
an occasional serious glut in the winter. Nine winters out of ten, oil
sells for $2/gallon in winter, one winter out of ten it sells for
$1/gallon. The circumstances that cause the glut (a warm winter, say) are
not predictable in the preceding summer. Ignoring interest and storage
costs for simplicity, the future price will be $1.90/gallon. On average
buying it in advance does neither better nor worse than buying it when you
need it--but nine times out of ten it does better.

My father's old (real) example for this was speculating on the Mexican
currency. You could almost always make money buying peso (I think that is
the name of the money) futures now and selling them when they came due. The
reason was that each year there was a small probability of a large
devaluation. So you had a high probability of a small gain, and a low
probability of a large loss.

Whether this has anything to do with Perry's experience, I of course do not
know, but it is a point worth remembering in evaluating evidence of this
sort.

