From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: INVEST: Corn futures (was Re: Efficient Market Hypothesis...)

The likely main reason winter oil futures are often cheaper in the 
summer is that advance buyers provide information on the winter's 
expected demand.  This allows producers to plan production ahead 
of time, based on a locked-in market and conservative extrapolation, 
instead of facing highly uncertain demand, which requires having to 
store summer's production, and/or massive hiring or laying off of workers 
during the winter.  The proceeds also provide capital for funding
such production.  Thus the discount reflects not only interest gained 
by the seller, but also great reduction in uncertainty about
their market.  Speculators and oil buyers who buy winter oil futures
in the summer and sell or use them in the winter are (a) taking
the high-beta risk off the backs of the producers and (b) reducing
it, when they know more about the expected demand.  They receive
a corresponding high return, for the beta plus the value of
their information (positive if the buyer knows better than the seller 
how much he is going to use, or the speculator knows better than the 
seller how much other people are going to use).  In general,
supply/demand messages are being sent via the lossy compression of the 
futures price, with both senders and receivers increasing their utility
in the process.

